\section{Blueprint Visualization and Video Export Subsystem}
\label{sec:blueprint}

\subsection{Overview}
Modern computational astrophysics workflows benefit from visualization
layers that provide immediate structural and diagnostic feedback during
simulation. We present a real-time blueprint visualization subsystem
designed as an interactive front-end for autonomous simulation engines,
relativistic $N$-body frameworks, and multi-scale dynamical systems.
The subsystem renders schematic geometry, telemetry overlays, and
diagnostic scan-lines at 60\,fps, and includes a browser-native video
export pipeline for reproducible archival.

\subsection{System Architecture}
The subsystem is implemented using the HTML5 Canvas API and a
JavaScript-based animation kernel. The rendering surface is a
1280\,$\times$\,720 canvas enclosed within a neon blueprint-style
interface. The architecture consists of the following components:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Blueprint Grid Layer:} A cyan vector grid providing
    geometric reference for schematic overlays and diagnostic elements.

    \item \textbf{Telemetry Header:} Real-time display of frame rate,
    resolution, and ISO timestamp for reproducibility.

    \item \textbf{Rotating Wireframe Geometry:} Dual-layer square
    wireframes rendered under continuous rotation to emulate isometric
    schematic motion.

    \item \textbf{Concentric Radar Rings:} Circular diagnostic elements
    emphasizing rotational symmetry and geometric centering.

    \item \textbf{Animated Scan-Line:} A horizontal laser-style sweep
    with positional readout, useful for visual diagnostics.

    \item \textbf{Video Capture Pipeline:} A 60\,fps WebM recording
    engine using \texttt{canvas.captureStream()} and VP9 encoding.
\end{itemize}

The subsystem operates independently of the underlying simulation
kernel, enabling integration with relativistic dynamics engines,
chaos-map generators, and federated GPU clusters.

\subsection{Rendering Kernel}
The rendering kernel is driven by \texttt{requestAnimationFrame},
ensuring synchronization with the browser refresh cycle. Two state
variables govern the animation:

\begin{align}
    \theta(t) &= \theta_0 + 0.015\,t, \\
    y_{\mathrm{scan}}(t) &= (3t) \bmod 720.
\end{align}

Each frame executes the following sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Clear the canvas and draw the blueprint grid.
    \item Render telemetry text in cyan monospace type.
    \item Draw radar rings at fixed radii.
    \item Apply rotation and render wireframe geometry.
    \item Draw the animated scan-line and its positional label.
\end{enumerate}

The deterministic nature of the kernel ensures that identical input
states produce identical visual outputs, satisfying reproducibility
requirements for scientific visualization.

\subsection{Video Capture and Export Pipeline}
The subsystem includes a browser-native video capture engine operating
at 60\,fps. The capture stream is initialized via:

\begin{verbatim}
const stream = canvas.captureStream(60);
const mediaRecorder = new MediaRecorder(stream, {
    mimeType: 'video/webm;codecs=vp9'
});
\end{verbatim}

Captured chunks are assembled into a Blob and exported as
\texttt{blueprint\_schematic\_render.webm}. The recording duration is
fixed at 5\,s, ensuring consistent output length for archival,
comparison, and supplementary material in journal submissions.

\subsection{Performance Characteristics}
The subsystem maintains a stable 60\,fps rendering rate on modern
hardware. The blueprint grid, wireframe geometry, and scan-line
animation are computationally lightweight, enabling smooth operation
even when embedded within larger simulation dashboards or multi-cluster
control interfaces.

The video export pipeline introduces negligible overhead during
recording, as encoding is performed asynchronously by the browser's
media subsystem.

\subsection{Integration with Autonomous Simulation Engines}
The blueprint renderer integrates seamlessly with autonomous simulation
architectures such as relativistic $N$-body engines, chaos atlas
generators, and federated GPU clusters. Its deterministic rendering loop
makes it suitable for:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Real-time visualization of simulation states.
    \item Diagnostic overlays for stability classification.
    \item Schematic rendering of system components.
    \item Generation of reproducible video artifacts for publication.
\end{itemize}

The subsystem may operate as a standalone module or as a front-end
component within multi-cluster orchestration frameworks.

\subsection{Discussion}
The blueprint visualization subsystem provides a reproducible and
scientifically robust method for rendering schematic designs and
simulation diagnostics. Its deterministic animation kernel, neon
blueprint aesthetic, and autonomous video export capabilities make it a
valuable component in computational astrophysics workflows.

Future extensions may include WebGL-based 3D rendering, real-time
interaction with simulation data streams, and automated annotation
overlays driven by autonomous diagnostic engines.

